WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:20.017
The next morning, when Mary arrived in Colin's room, she saw that he looked pale and worn out

2
00:00:20.077 --> 00:00:21.178
from his temper tantrum.

3
00:00:22.459 --> 00:00:23.360
"I'm glad you came.

4
00:00:24.481 --> 00:00:26.943
I ache all over because I'm so tired."

5
00:00:28.244 --> 00:00:29.946
He noticed she was wearing her hat.

6
00:00:29.966 --> 00:00:32.328
"Are you going somewhere?"

7
00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:34.772
"I won't be long.

8
00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:36.973
I need to talk to Dickon.

9
00:00:38.134 --> 00:00:39.354
It's something about the garden."

10
00:00:39.374 --> 00:00:41.115
"Oh, is it?"

11
00:00:41.495 --> 00:00:42.496
Colin said eagerly.

12
00:00:42.516 --> 00:00:45.337
"I dreamed about the garden all night."

13
00:00:47.458 --> 00:00:49.038
Mary left him and went outside.

14
00:00:50.239 --> 00:00:52.360
Dickon was already at work in the secret garden.

15
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.581
His animal friends were busy watching him.

16
00:00:56.822 --> 00:01:01.844
Besides the fox and crow, he'd brought along two squirrels named Nut and Shell.

17
00:01:03.464 --> 00:01:06.967
"I rode over on Jump, my moor pony," he told Mary.

18
00:01:06.987 --> 00:01:09.169
"Would you like to see him?"

19
00:01:11.531 --> 00:01:15.395
Dickon led Mary into the woods, where the shaggy pony was eating grass.

20
00:01:16.736 --> 00:01:19.298
The boy and his pony seemed to have a conversation.

21
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.382
Then Dickon made Jump give Mary a kiss on her cheek.

22
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:25.424
She liked that.

23
00:01:26.805 --> 00:01:29.708
Back in the garden, the two children sat under a tree.

24
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.622
Mary began to tell Dickon about Colin's tantrum.

25
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:36.624
Dickon shook his head sadly.

26
00:01:36.644 --> 00:01:39.526
"Just listen to the birds this morning.

27
00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:44.030
The world seems full of them, all whistling and singing.

28
00:01:45.331 --> 00:01:50.314
And that poor boy has been lying shut up in the house, thinking thoughts that made him scream.

29
00:01:51.615 --> 00:01:54.938
We must get him out here, soaking up air and sunshine.

30
00:01:56.279 --> 00:01:57.720
We have no time to lose."

31
00:01:59.204 --> 00:02:01.066
"No, there is no time to lose."

32
00:02:02.887 --> 00:02:06.790
The two of them agreed upon a plan, and then Mary ran back into the house.

33
00:02:08.191 --> 00:02:12.113
When she sat down next to Colin's bed, he sniffed the air nearby.

34
00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:15.616
"You smell like flowers and fresh things!"

35
00:02:15.636 --> 00:02:20.419
"That's the wind from the moor—and the springtime and sunshine."

36
00:02:22.101 --> 00:02:23.622
Colin took another big breath.

37
00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:26.464
There was so much to talk about.

38
00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.901
He wanted to hear all about Dickon's wild animal friends.

39
00:02:30.921 --> 00:02:33.763
"Well," said Mary.

40
00:02:33.783 --> 00:02:37.664
"Today I met Jump, the moor pony.

41
00:02:38.905 --> 00:02:42.106
He trotted right up to Dickon and laid his head on Dickon's shoulder.

42
00:02:43.327 --> 00:02:47.269
Dickon talked into his ear, and Jump answered back with whinnies and snorts!"

43
00:02:49.009 --> 00:02:51.991
"Do you think Jump really understands everything Dickon says?"

44
00:02:52.011 --> 00:02:54.592
"It seems as if he does.

45
00:02:55.852 --> 00:02:58.614
Dickon says anything will understand you if you're friends with it."

46
00:02:59.975 --> 00:03:01.837
Colin lay quietly for a little while.

47
00:03:03.158 --> 00:03:06.641
He seemed to be staring at the wall, but Mary knew he was thinking.

48
00:03:06.661 --> 00:03:11.505
"I wish I were friends with things," he said at last.

49
00:03:11.525 --> 00:03:13.486
"But I'm not.

50
00:03:14.787 --> 00:03:18.550
I never had anything to be friends with, and I can't stand people."

51
00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.852
"Can you stand me?"

52
00:03:20.872 --> 00:03:23.014
"Yes, I can.

53
00:03:23.494 --> 00:03:25.296
It's funny, but I even like you."

54
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.201
Mary was thoughtful too.

55
00:03:28.221 --> 00:03:33.064
"Ben Weatherstaff, the gardener, once said that he and I were alike.

56
00:03:34.465 --> 00:03:35.846
I think you're like us too.

57
00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:40.930
Ben said neither of us was good-looking, and we were as sour as we looked.

58
00:03:42.231 --> 00:03:45.893
But I don't feel so sour anymore, now that I know Dickon and the robin."

59
00:03:45.913 --> 00:03:49.396
"Did you feel as though you hated people?"

60
00:03:49.416 --> 00:03:52.197
"Yes," said Mary.

61
00:03:52.218 --> 00:03:55.560
"I would have hated you if I'd seen you back then."

62
00:03:56.916 --> 00:03:59.117
Colin reached out his hand and patted her.

63
00:03:59.137 --> 00:04:04.140
"Mary, I wish I hadn't said what I did about sending Dickon away.

64
00:04:05.421 --> 00:04:06.782
I wouldn't mind him looking at me.

65
00:04:08.022 --> 00:04:08.763
I want to see him."

66
00:04:08.783 --> 00:04:15.326
"I'm glad you said that because because "Because what?"

67
00:04:15.727 --> 00:04:16.727
Colin said eagerly.

68
00:04:18.228 --> 00:04:22.651
Mary was so anxious that she hopped up from her stool and caught hold of both his hands.

69
00:04:23.951 --> 00:04:24.812
"Can I trust you?

70
00:04:26.048 --> 00:04:27.629
Can I really trust you?"

71
00:04:27.649 --> 00:04:30.369
"Yes! Yes!"

72
00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:34.350
"Dickon will bring his creatures to see you tomorrow.

73
00:04:35.571 --> 00:04:36.671
But that's not all."

74
00:04:38.032 --> 00:04:39.812
Mary was shaking with excitement.

75
00:04:39.832 --> 00:04:43.493
"There's a door into the secret garden.

76
00:04:44.673 --> 00:04:46.634
I found it under the ivy on the wall."

77
00:04:48.314 --> 00:04:53.176
If he had been a strong, healthy boy, Colin probably would have jumped up and shouted, "Hooray!"

78
00:04:53.788 --> 00:04:57.071
Instead his eyes grew bigger and he gasped for breath.

79
00:04:57.091 --> 00:04:59.072
"Oh, Mary!

80
00:04:59.592 --> 00:05:00.253
Will I see it?"

81
00:05:01.394 --> 00:05:02.514
He pulled her toward him.

82
00:05:03.695 --> 00:05:05.176
"Will I live to get inside it?"

83
00:05:06.858 --> 00:05:09.640
"Of course you'll see it," Mary said impatiently.

84
00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.802
"Of course you'll live to get inside it.

85
00:05:14.103 --> 00:05:15.023
Don't be silly."

86
00:05:16.425 --> 00:05:20.227
She seemed so certain about this that he soon began to laugh at himself.

87
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.082
He asked her to tell him again about the garden and how it might look.

88
00:05:26.442 --> 00:05:30.424
He listened to every detail and soon forgot his aches and tiredness.

89
00:05:30.444 --> 00:05:34.806
"It sounds as if you've really seen it," he said.

90
00:05:36.187 --> 00:05:39.608
Mary hesitated for a minute and then blurted out the truth.

91
00:05:39.628 --> 00:05:43.390
"I found the key and went into the garden weeks ago.

92
00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:47.952
But I didn't dare tell you until I knew for sure that I could trust you."
